# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 has nothing from it; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.3 have "feel_effect"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the devotees feel the good effect of external motions, postures and actions in enlivening their devotion and quickening their fervour, which would otherwise decay if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects" is false. Is S2, "The devotees of that superstition usually plead in excuse for the mummeries, with which they are upbraided, that they feel the good effect of those external motions, and postures, and actions, in enlivening their devotion and quickening their fervour, which otherwise would decay, if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects.", true then?
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 has nothing from it; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.3 have "images_present"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "shadowing out the objects of faith in sensible types and images renders them more present than is possible merely by an intellectual view and contemplation" is false. Is S3, "We shadow out the objects of our faith, say they, in sensible types and images, and render them more present to us by the immediate presence of these types, than it is possible for us to do merely by an intellectual view and contemplation.", true then?
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1 has "feel_effect" and "images_present" and "concur"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1.3 have "concur"; they differ when only "the devotees feel the good effect of external motions, postures and actions in enlivening their devotion and quickening their fervour, which would otherwise decay if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects" and "in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur" are true, which the second allows
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the devotees feel the good effect of external motions, postures and actions in enlivening their devotion and quickening their fervour, which would otherwise decay if directed entirely to distant and immaterial objects" and "in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur" are true and "shadowing out the objects of faith in sensible types and images renders them more present than is possible merely by an intellectual view and contemplation" is false. Is S5, "I shall only infer from these practices, and this reasoning, that the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common; and as in every case a resemblance and a present impression must concur, we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle.", true then?
